riod': 'announced July 1, 2026', 'status': 'happened'}, 'weight': 'supporting'}\nseed keys ['company', 'as_of', 'about', 'questions']\ndict_keys(['view_id', 'object_id', 'object_kind', 'kind', 'looks_at', 'question', 'due_day', 'year', 'rungs', 'key', 'depends_on', 'ladder_status', 'source'])\nseed 1 What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027? [(500000000000.0, None, 'FY2027 total revenue is at least $500 billion.'), (400000000000.0, 500000000000.0, 'FY2027 total revenue is at least $400 billion and below $500 billion.'), (350000000000.0, 400000000000.0, 'FY2027 total revenue is at least $350 billion and below $400 billion.'), (300000000000.0, 350000000000.0, 'FY2027 total revenue is at least $300 billion and below $350 billion.'), (None, 300000000000.0, 'FY2027 total revenue is below $300 billion.')]\nseed 2 What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027? [(440000000000.0, None, 'FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $440 billion.'), (360000000000.0, 440000000000.0, 'at least $360 billion and below $440 billion.'), (310000000000.0, 360000000000.0, 'at least $310 billion and below $360 billion.'), (260000000000.0, 310000000000.0, 'at least $260 billion and below $310 billion.'), (None, 260000000000.0, 'below $260 billion.')]\nseed 19 How much revenue will NVIDIA report from standalone Vera CPUs in FY2027? [(20000000000.0, None, 'FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion.'), (12000000000.0, 20000000000.0, 'FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue is at least $12 billion and below $20 billion.'), (6000000000.0, 12000000000.0, 'FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue is at least $6 billion and below $12 billion.'), (2000000000.0, 6000000000.0, 'FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue is at least $2 billion and below $6 billion.'), (None, 2000000000.0, 'FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue is below $2 billion.')]\nseed 22 What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2028? [(650000000000.0, None, 'FY2028 total revenue is at least $650 billion.'), (550000000000.0, 650000000000.0, 'FY2028 total revenue is at least $550 billion and below $650 billion.'), (480000000000.0, 550000000000.0, 'FY2028 total revenue is at least $480 billion and below $550 billion.'), (420000000000.0, 480000000000.0, 'FY2028 total revenue is at least $420 billion and below $480 billion.'), (None, 420000000000.0, 'FY2028 total revenue is below $420 billion.')]\npacket\n{'id': 'i037', 'day': '2026-07-02', 'title': 'Microsoft vs. Nvidia: Which Is the Better AI Stock to Own for the Next 3 Years?', 'input_id': 'gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b', 'tier': 'third_party_view', 'who': 'Wall Street analysts (consensus, as reported by the author)', 'passage': 'from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year', 'meaning': {'who': 'Wall Street analysts', 'what': 'NVIDIA total revenue estimates', 'how_much': '$392 billion this fiscal year, $554 billion next year (from $253 billion trailing twelve months)', 'period': 'this fiscal year (FY2027) and the following year (FY2028)', 'status': 'forecast'}, 'weight': 'core'}\n{'id': 'i090', 'day': '2026-07-04', 'title': 'Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?', 'input_id': 'gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568', 'tier': 'third_party_view', 'who': 'Wall Street analysts (consensus, as reported by the author)', 'passage': 'Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year', 'meaning': {'who': 'Wall Street analysts', 'what': 'NVIDIA revenue growth forecast', 'how_much': '82% this year; 41% next year', 'period': 'this year and next year (fiscal or calendar both accepted, D233)', 'status': 'forecast'}, 'weight': 'core'}\n{'id': 'i095', 'day': '2026-07-05', 'title': \"AMD Stock and Intel Crushed Nvidia in the First Half. Here's My Prediction for the Second Half.\", 'input_id': 'gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348', 'tier': 'party_statement', 'who': 'NVIDIA (management forecast, as reported by the author)', 'passage': 'Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year', 'meaning': {'who': 'NVIDIA', 'what': 'stand-alone CPU sales forecast', 'how_much': '$20 billion', 'period': 'this year', 'status': 'forecast'}, 'weight': 'core'}\n{'id': 'i096', 'day': '2026-07-05', 'title': \"AMD Stock and Intel Crushed Nvidia in the First Half. Here's My Prediction for the Second Half.\", 'input_id': 'gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348', 'tier': 'party_statement', 'who': 'NVIDIA (product schedule, as reported by the author)', 'passage': \"it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform\", 'meaning': {'who': 'NVIDIA', 'what': 'first stand-alone CPU (Vera) launches as part of the Vera Rubin platform', 'period': 'fall 2026', 'status': 'plan'}, 'weight': 'core'}\n{'id': 'i129', 'day': '2026-07-06', 'title': 'NVIDIA Falls 7% in a Month: Should You Still Hold NVDA Stock or Exit?', 'input_id': 'gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a', 'tier': 'fact', 'who': 'NVIDIA (Q1 FY2027 results, restated by Zacks)', 'passage': 'During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales.', 'meaning': {'who': 'NVIDIA reported', 'what': 'Q1 FY2027 revenue, data center revenue, non-GAAP EPS', 'how_much': 'revenue $81.62B (+85% y/y); data center $75.25B (+92% y/y, +21% q/q, 92% of sales); EPS $1.87', 'period': 'Q1 FY2027', 'status': 'happened'}, 'weight': 'optional'}\n{'id': 'i173', 'day': '2026-07-06', 'title': \"Nvidia Stock Is Below $200 Per Share Again. Here's When It Could Hit $300.\", 'input_id': 'gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f', 'tier': 'party_statement', 'who': 'NVIDIA management (last quarterly conference call; call date not given in the article)', 'passage': 'management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026', 'meaning': {'who': 'NVIDIA management', 'what': 'hyperscaler (customer) spending forecast; not NVIDIA revenue', 'how_much': 'over $1 trillion (2027); about $650 billion projected (2026)', 'period': '2026 and 2027', 'status': 'forecast'}, 'weight': 'core'}\n{'id': 'i174', 'day': '2026-07-06', 'title': \"Nvidia Stock Is Below $200 Per Share Again. Here's When It Could Hit $300.\", 'input_id': 'gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f', 'tier': 'third_party_view', 'who': 'Wall Street analysts (as reported)', 'passage': 'For the rest of this year, they expect 82% growth. For 2027, that figure rises to 41%.', 'meaning': {'who': 'Wall Street analysts', 'what': \"NVIDIA revenue growth forecast ('revenue' is in the preceding sentence and must survive)\", 'how_much': '82% (rest of this year); 41% (2027)', 'period': 'rest of this year (not the full year); 2027 (fiscal or calendar basis unstated; either accepted, D233)', 'status': 'forecast'}, 'weight': 'core'}\n{'id': 'i218', 'day': '2026-07-06', 'title': 'Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why', 'input_id': 'gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd', 'tier': 'third_party_view', 'who': 'Morgan Stanley Research', 'passage': 'The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.', 'meaning': {'who': 'Morgan Stanley Research (an estimate)', 'what': \"NVIDIA's selling price per Rubin GPU and per Vera CPU\", 'how_much': 'about $55,000 per GPU; $5,000 per CPU', 'condition': 'when sold in volume', 'status': 'forecast'}, 'weight': 'core'}\n{'id': 'i256', 'day': '2026-07-07', 'title': 'Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?', 'input_id': 'gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c', 'tier': 'party_statement', 'who': 'the big four hyperscalers (2026 plans, summarised by the author); NVIDIA (2027 claim)', 'passage': 'This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.', 'meaning': {'who': \"big four AI hyperscalers' own plans (2026); NVIDIA's claim (2027)\", 'what': 'data-center capital expenditure of the big four', 'how_much': 'around $650bn (2026); more than $1 trillion (2027)', 'period': 'calendar 2026; calendar 2027', 'status': 'plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)'}, 'weight': 'core'}\n{'id': 'i280', 'day': '2026-07-09', 'title': 'DeepSeek Just Dealt Nvidia Stock a Potentially Major Blow', 'input_id': 'gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e', 'tier': 'fact', 'who': 'NVIDIA (Q1 FY2027 report of May 20, restated by Barchart)', 'passage': 'Nvidia posted record revenue of $81.6 billion, an 85% year-over-year (YOY) increase', 'meaning': {'who': 'NVIDIA', 'what': 'Q1 FY2027 revenue, net income, EPS, gross margin, Data Center and networking revenue', 'how_much': 'revenue $81.6 billion (+85%); Data Center $75.2 billion (+92%)', 'period': 'quarter ended April 26, 2026', 'status': 'happened'}, 'weight': 'optional'}\n{'id': 'i281', 'day': '2026-07-09', 'title': 'DeepSeek Just Dealt Nvidia Stock a Potentially Major Blow', 'input_id': 'gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e', 'tier': 'party_statement', 'who': 'NVIDIA (Q2 FY2027 guidance given May 20, restated by Barchart)', 'passage': 'forecasting revenue of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%, and a non-GAAP gross margin of about 75%', 'meaning': {'who': 'NVIDIA', 'what': 'Q2 FY2027 revenue and gross-margin guidance', 'how_much': '$91 billion ±2%; non-GAAP gross margin about 75%', 'period': 'Q2 FY2027', 'condition': 'assumes no China Data Center compute revenue', 'status': 'forecast'}, 'weight': 'supporting'}\n{'id': 'i288', 'day': '2026-07-10', 'title': 'TD Cowen Names Nvidia Stock a Top Pick on Massive Unpriced AI Upside', 'input_id': 'gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c', 'tier': 'party_statement', 'who': 'NVIDIA (as reported by TIKR)', 'passage': 'its Vera CPU line, which the company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year', 'meaning': {'who': 'NVIDIA', 'what': 'sales of its stand-alone Vera CPU line', 'how_much': '$20 billion', 'period': 'by the end of this fiscal year (FY2027)', 'status': 'forecast'}, 'weight': 'core'}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'from pathlib import Path\nimport json,re,sqlite3\ndef gets(run,call):\n p=Path(f'\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-{run}/prod/archive.sqlite'\\\\'').resolve()\n c=sqlite3.connect(p.as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True);c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n r=c.execute('\\\\''select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(call,)).fetchone()\n req=json.loads(r['\\\\''request_json'\\\\'']); ans=json.loads(r['\\\\''answer_json'\\\\''])\n return req['\\\\''messages'\\\\''][-1]['\\\\''content'\\\\''],ans\nsamples=[(2,8729,'\\\\''e2499|e4762'\\\\''),(2,8737,'\\\\''e2848|e3219|e976'\\\\''),(2,8734,'\\\\''e2620|e2889|e3474|e4648|e3934'\\\\''),(2,8595,'\\\\''e1786|e2535|e1926'\\\\''),(3,9166,'\\\\''e3660|e4856|e5399'\\\\'')]\nfor run,call,ids in samples:\n t,ans=gets(run,call)\n print(call,ans['\\\\''rung'\\\\''],ans['\\\\''reference'\\\\''],ans['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''])\n for l in re.findall(rf'\\\\''\"'^- (?:{ids}) '\"\\\\[.*\\\\n    Quote:.*\"'$'\"'\\\\'',t,re.M):print(l)\n print()\ndef theme(t):\n a=t.split('\\\\''[Themes in force,'\\\\'',1)\n return a[1].split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\n[Previous conclusion'\\\\'',1)[0] if len(a)==2 else '\\\\'''\\\\''\nfor i,calls in [(1,(9014,9022)),(2,(8729,8737)),(3,(9183,9177))]:\n a,b=[theme(gets(i,n)[0]) for n in calls]\n print('\\\\''theme block same'\\\\'',i,a==b)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'from pathlib import Path\nimport json,re,sqlite3\ndef gets(run,call):\n p=Path(f'\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-{run}/prod/archive.sqlite'\\\\'').resolve()\n c=sqlite3.connect(p.as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True);c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n r=c.execute('\\\\''select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(call,)).fetchone()\n req=json.loads(r['\\\\''request_json'\\\\'']); ans=json.loads(r['\\\\''answer_json'\\\\''])\n return req['\\\\''messages'\\\\''][-1]['\\\\''content'\\\\''],ans\nsamples=[(2,8729,'\\\\''e2499|e4762'\\\\''),(2,8737,'\\\\''e2848|e3219|e976'\\\\''),(2,8734,'\\\\''e2620|e2889|e3474|e4648|e3934'\\\\''),(2,8595,'\\\\''e1786|e2535|e1926'\\\\''),(3,9166,'\\\\''e3660|e4856|e5399'\\\\'')]\nfor run,call,ids in samples:\n t,ans=gets(run,call)\n print(call,ans['\\\\''rung'\\\\''],ans['\\\\''reference'\\\\''],ans['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''])\n for l in re.findall(rf'\\\\''\"'^- (?:{ids}) '\"\\\\[.*\\\\n    Quote:.*\"'$'\"'\\\\'',t,re.M):print(l)\n print()\ndef theme(t):\n a=t.split('\\\\''[Themes in force,'\\\\'',1)\n return a[1].split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\n[Previous conclusion'\\\\'',1)[0] if len(a)==2 else '\\\\'''\\\\''\nfor i,calls in [(1,(9014,9022)),(2,(8729,8737)),(3,(9183,9177))]:\n a,b=[theme(gets(i,n)[0]) for n in calls]\n print('\\\\''theme block same'\\\\'',i,a==b)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"8729 2 Market expectation is about $411 billion for FY2027, rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion). Zacks' 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of approximately $215.9 billion implies about $405 billion, also rung 2. About $419 billion: Q1 $81.6 billion plus Q2 $96.2 billion, estimated Q3 of about $113.5 billion (the $110.16 billion guidance high end plus the reported average 3.1% beat), and Q4 of about $127 billion by carrying forward the guided 12% sequential growth.\n- e2499 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 revenue to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, assuming no Data Center compute revenue from China.\n    Quote: Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook.\n- e4762 [third_party_view; Manali Pradhan, CFA; original source Manali Pradhan, CFA 2026-09-15; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] Manali Pradhan said NVIDIA exceeded the high end of revenue guidance in each of the past 12 quarters, averaging 3.1% above the high end over the past four quarters.\n    Quote: Nvidia has exceeded the high end of its revenue guidance in each of the past 12 quarters. Over the past four quarters, actual revenue has averaged 3.1% above the high end of guidance.\n\n8737 2 The market reference supplied by the previous conclusion is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2 ($400 billion to below $450 billion). Approximately $410 billion: $164 billion reported in the first half, about $100 billion implied for Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and roughly $146 billion estimated for Q4. The Q4 estimate reflects management's expected hyperscale reacceleration and Rubin ramp, with a discounted contribution from SemiAnalysis's forecast that second-half Data Center revenue would exceed analyst estimates by 20%.\n- e976 [third_party_view; fool.com author Lyle Daly; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:bdd09874d53aef7b27866d44 2026-07-26] SemiAnalysis forecast that NVIDIA Data Center revenue in the second half of FY2027 would exceed analyst estimates by 20%.\n    Quote: And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\n- e2848 [party_statement; techcrunch.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:d949d26fe875780037064b8a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided to third-quarter revenue of $108 billion, with some revenue expected from Rubin GPUs.\n    Quote: Nvidia said it expects revenue to reach $108 billion in the third quarter, some of which will come from its next-gen Rubin GPUs.\n- e3219 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source fool.com (Daniel Sparks) 2026-08-28; from gnews:387b486b6e29491b9c8506df 2026-08-28] Daniel Sparks calculated that FY2027 Q3 Data Center revenue would be $99.9 billion if its share of total revenue stayed at FY2027 Q2's 92.5%.\n    Quote: Hold the data center mix at last quarter's 92.5%, and data center revenue works out to $99.9 billion -- about $100 million short of the line.\n\n8734 3 Bank of America's roughly $70 billion estimate of NVIDIA's equity investments in ecosystem partners; this is rung 3 ($50 billion to below $75 billion). Its period and whether it measures outstanding commitments are unclear. About $53 billion: $25 billion of commitments reported as of July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027, then the $3.5 billion MediaTek investment announced after that remainder-of-year figure and a risk-adjusted contribution from the $10 billion Anthropic and $2.5 billion Thinking Machines discussions.\n- e2620 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000075:nvda-20260726.htm 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported $99 billion of equity investments and $25 billion of equity investment commitments as of 2026-07-26.\n    Quote: These include equity investments of $99 billion and equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026.\n- e2889 [party_statement; Business Insider, reporting NVIDIA's statement; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:aec1fae1b903de5c43ba7fdf 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said on Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the remainder of its fiscal year, in addition to $47.9 billion held in private companies.\n    Quote: The company said Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year, on top of $47.9 billion it already holds in private companies.\n- e3474 [fact; insidermonkey.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:baa07a74334dc91adc7d85bc 2026-08-31] NVIDIA is investing $3.5 billion in MediaTek through convertible bonds.\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is investing $3.5 billion in Taiwan-based MediaTek through convertible bonds, taking an existing AI partnership a step further.\n- e3934 [rumour; finance.yahoo.com; original source finance.yahoo.com ; from gnews:67a905d722fae62095fd741e 2026-09-04] NVIDIA is reportedly discussing an approximately $2.5 billion investment in Thinking Machines Lab; no completion date is stated.\n    Quote: Nvidia is separately in discussions to invest approximately $2.5 billion\n- e4648 [rumour; Calcalist Tech, citing Reuters; original source Unnamed people familiar with the discussions cited by Reuters ; from gnews:767d795fbdf40bc90ce11e15 2026-09-14] People familiar with discussions told Reuters that Nvidia could invest up to $10 billion in Anthropic’s potential IPO.\n    Quote: Nvidia could invest as much as $10 billion\n\n8595 4 Calendar-2027 hyperscaler spending consensus is about $1.08 trillion (e1926), in rung 3 ($900 billion to below $1.1 trillion). About $1.19 trillion for the four named hyperscalers: apply the prior approximate $730 billion/$800 billion four-to-five share proxy to NVIDIA’s $1.3 trillion top-five calendar-2027 CapEx estimate (e2535; repeated in e4756 and later reports).\n- e1786 [third_party_view; Patrick Sanders, The Motley Fool; original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they expect combined AI-infrastructure spending to exceed $730 billion in calendar 2026 and increase in calendar 2027.\n    Quote: Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year, and they expect that number to increase next year.\n- e1926 [third_party_view; uk.finance.yahoo.com, citing Bank of America; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Bank of America said analyst consensus for hyperscalers’ calendar 2027 spending rose from $480 billion in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion in August 2026, an increase of about 127%.\n    Quote: What analysts expect the hyperscalers to spend in 2027 alone has more than doubled in the space of a year, from a consensus of $480 billion (€416bn) in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion (€943bn) this month, a rise of about 127%, according to Bank of America.\n- e2535 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said cloud-industry backlog exceeded $2 trillion and top-five hyperscaler capital expenditures were expected to approach $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n\n9166 3 NVIDIA management’s stated expectation that FY2028 CPU revenue will more than double the $20 billion FY2027 expectation implies more than $40 billion, which is rung 2. Roughly $38–39 billion for FY2028: the stated more-than-double expectation points above $40 billion, but is discounted for a forward Vera target without shipment volume in hand, with the competitive evidence adding execution risk.\n- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n- e5399 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026.\n    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n\ntheme block same 1 True\ntheme block same 2 True\ntheme block same 3 True\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"NOT READY\n\nThe gate can pass after catching the **$146B segment forecast against a $127B company total**, while leaving the mismatched Q3 bases unchecked. It can also reject the frozen gold’s valid way of deriving a market comparison. Those are failures of the proposed format and checks.\n\n1. **C4 does not identify the shared forecast, and C3 can miss the quarter.** In calls **8729/8737**, the $108B guide and the $113.5B beat-adjusted forecast are both legitimate figures. Giving them different `measure` names lets both cards use different Q3 bases without a C4 conflict. The schema also lacks a scenario ID and a requirement to bind the quarters actually used in each answer. An annual-only reconstruction can show $410B below $419B and hide the Q4 contradiction. **Fix I’d accept:** keep `revenue` as the measure. Bind each relevant figure to a canonical scope, period, basis, role, and declared scenario. Require the answers to use the same forecast node for a shared quarter within a scenario. Declare which quantities C3 compares and how paired ranges are checked. In R1, exercise the Q4 contradiction and Q3-base conflict independently, with a valid guide-plus-forecast control. [Design §§2–3 and 5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:22)\n\n2. **The format cannot faithfully express all four gold controls.** There is one family-wide `reference`, but **q01** uses $392B of outside consensus and **q02** derives its own comparison from $392B × 92%. C5 refuses that derived reference. **q22** needs to reuse q01’s FY2027 forecast even though §1 limits families to one fiscal period. **q19** exposes a separate problem: its $8–18B range overlaps two seeded bands, yet C6 forces the band containing the $13B midpoint. The midpoint says nothing about which band the analyst thinks is likelier. That is an importance rule, and the frozen scorer uses overlap. **Fix I’d accept:** give each answer its own direct, externally derived, or unavailable comparison. Permit a specifically declared cross-period base and keep it labelled as a system forecast when used that way. Preserve the arithmetic intermediate and the analyst’s final range as distinct figures. For a range, code checks that the model’s chosen band overlaps it. The model explains its choice. Keep the frozen scorer as written. [Design §§1–3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:6), [Frozen gold](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json), [Frozen scoring rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/score_judgement.py:1)\n\n3. **The two recorded lists cannot reliably expose R2–R4.** The overlap list has no input-level relationship to inspect. R2 can put the entire $53B under `estimate` and bypass the two-source `sum` rule. In R3, `730 ÷ 800 × 1,300` can pass C1 and C2 with correctly distinct C4 keys and no estimate node at all. In R4, removing $38–39B removes the outcome itself. There is no general neutral value for a forecast range, a discount, or growth carried forward. **Fix I’d accept:** require the calculation to name the overlap or scope assumption at the operation that uses it. Record sensitivity only against an explicit alternate calculation. R2 can show approximately $35B **if the disputed $18B increment is omitted and the other assumptions are held fixed**. R3 must expose the scope bridge and can report sensitivity as undetermined. R4 can show that the trim crosses below $40B relative to the stated undiscounted lower bound, without inventing an exact undiscounted forecast. Code records the arithmetic and the missing relationships. A reader decides whether the assumption is defensible. D721 and D722 also rule out treating unresolved inclusion as proof of double counting. [Design §3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:51), [D721–D722](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:10859)\n\n4. **C1 checks nearby tokens, not the quantity the source stated.** In **8729**, `e2499` gives **$108B ±2%**. The quoted inputs are $108B and 2%. The $105.84–110.16B interval is computed. In **8595**, `e1786` says **more than $730B**, and `e2535` says **nearly $800B**. Neither supplies the exact point used in the ratio. In **9166**, `e3660` says **more than double**, a bound expressed in words. A blanket 0.5% tolerance would also accept $108.5B as a direct quote of $108B. **Fix I’d accept:** bind a source figure to a specific span of the shown quote, then match its value, unit, sign, and qualifier after exact normalization. Define the accepted forms for points, ranges, bounds, relative tolerances, and supported words such as “double.” Keep other wording visible as evidence. Compute endpoints and display rounding separately. Exercise these real passages in the gate, including a wrong number that falls inside the proposed tolerance. [Design C1–C2](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:53), [Old-2 archive](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/archive.sqlite), [Old-3 archive](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-3/prod/archive.sqlite)\n\n5. **§5 can pass without proving the checks work on the registered cases.** R1 accepts *either* C3 or C4, and R3 can pass by failing C4 even though the problem is a conversion between distinct quantities. The tables also leave open whether a response can omit or duplicate a member, point a card at another member’s outcome, use a dangling or circular figure reference, or report insufficient evidence directly. **Fix I’d accept:** require a named finding for each registered defect, with control reconstructions that preserve the gold’s actual reasoning. Keep the three plants and require each to fail for its intended reason. In the harness tables, specify exact family-member coverage, outcome ownership, valid figure references and operation types, the fixed as-of source, and the records written on success, unresolved, refusal, and incomplete execution. Give the model an explicit unresolved answer. Keep production lifecycle outside this gate. D726 leaves the first-release bundle change for release work and requires Alex’s ceiling before the paid gate. [Design §§4–5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:72), [Registered test](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RESULT.md:78), [D726](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:10991)\n\n6. **Appending a format block leaves conflicting instructions in the v13 request.** Judging-v7 tells one question to return one reference and outcome, tells the judge to read dependencies, and says the previous conclusion carries hidden evidence. The family design changes all three. **Fix I’d accept:** write and audit the full v13 request, including the family header and schema descriptions. Replace the old delivery and output instructions where they conflict, preserve D260/D263’s forecast reasoning, and show where each changed instruction comes from. Report the size change. That audit can be done with stored requests at $0. [Judging-v7 prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/prompts/judging-v7.txt:3), [Design §1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:18), [AGENTS §6](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:194)"}}
